From da614c520063f6aec14fd7e5422f23446e4b5f93 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 14:43:06 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 108/206] feat(shorebird): analyze_snapshot --shorebird
 subgraph_hash

SubgraphHasher's walk, recovered from their analyze_snapshot rather than
guessed. Their constructor names its three Hashers as SSO literals --
"subgraph" (this+0x28), "object_pool" (this+0x60) and "self" (this+0x98) --
so the three JSON hashes are identified without inference, and what
separates them is which entry kinds each vector receives.

The walk: AccumulateRecursive pushes OffsetFromPayloadStart, then tests a
vector<bool> at this+0x00 indexed by the entry's index in the instructions
table and returns if the bit is set. So the bound is a per-root visited
set, not a depth limit -- the `int depth` argument only gates the "self"
hasher (every push to this+0xb0 sits behind cbnz w21) and the log indent.
Recursion is at the call site, in instruction order.

"subgraph" is the hasher that does not classify: one RawInstructionBits
entry per word, and it keeps every word of a multi-instruction form --
the writes at 0x100197448 and 0x1001974ec are pops, and they pop
"object_pool" and "self" only. It also gets no CodeName; that entry is
pushed once by the constructor into this+0x78.

Reproduces the subgraph_hash of all 1380 functions in their own base.so
as digests, and our binary agrees with an independent ELF-driven model on
all 1589 of ours. It has to be digests: their 1380 values are all
distinct, so a partition comparison would be vacuous here.

op_subgraph_hash stays absent. Its routing is solved too, but it needs the
classified stream of every function in the subgraph, and that classifier
is right for 1208 of their 1380 -- so it reproduces only 842.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

